cartridge "SCORPION II/Darrell G. Rose/1988"

' SCORPION II
' A ColecoVision game based on "Scorpion II" by Darrell G. Rose,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette, October 1988 (Commodore 64, machine language,
' SYS 6051; also on the Best of 1988 disk with its article). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The game (from the article and the disassembly, orig/):
' - All 52 cards are dealt, no stock: seven columns of seven cards, and
'   three more under the middle three columns. Face down (18 cards): the
'   middle five of row 1, middle three of row 2, middle one of row 3, and
'   the same again in rows 5-7 (rows 4 and 8 face up), as the program's
'   pattern table at $0874.
' - Pick any face-up card, even one deep in a column. The program moves
'   it for you: if it is the last card of its column and its suit's home
'   pile wants it (an ace, then 2, 3 ... of that suit), it goes home, and
'   the next cards of that column follow while they are face up and
'   continue the same suit. Otherwise it goes, with every card below it
'   (the scorpion's tail, face-down ones included), onto the column whose
'   last card is the same suit and one rank higher. A king that is not
'   already first in its column goes to the first empty column. Anything
'   else is refused with a buzz.
' - When a column's last card is face down after a move, it turns up.
' - When no move is left the game ends: a win if all 52 cards are home,
'   otherwise a loss (the chime). Giving up counts as a loss. The main
'   menu keeps the wins and losses, and can deal a new shuffle or replay
'   the same cards.
'
' Liberties:
' - The joystick cursor jumps from card to card (up and down a column
'   over its face-up cards, left and right to the nearest card of the
'   next column) instead of the free C64 pointer; the left button picks.
'   The right button asks GIVE UP (the original's Q).
' - The original's long-column trick (a column of more than 27 cards was
'   swapped with column 1 to fit the screen) is replaced by scrolling: a
'   column longer than 18 cards shows arrows and scrolls with the cursor.
' - The shuffle is new (a numbered deal from a 16-bit generator; the deal
'   number is shown, and SAME DEAL replays it). The C64 drew cards from
'   its CIA timer. A move counter is added.
' - The look is new: one-tile card strips fanned down each column (the
'   C64 drew snaking columns of overlapping bitmap cards), the moved card
'   glides as a sprite. Sounds and the title tune ("Sting in the Tail")
'   are new; the buzz and the end chime follow the original's.
'
' Controls: either controller; stick and side buttons only (no keypad).
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI and the
' display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune once.

